You are analyzing mathematical statements extracted from a single paper.
Your sole task is to find IMPLICIT dependencies — ones that are invisible to
command-based scanners and keyword heuristics.

━━━ What counts as implicit ━━━

Intra-paper (between statements in this paper):
  • A statement re-uses notation, a construction, or a defined object introduced
    in an earlier statement, with no \ref command and no proximity keyword.
  • A proof silently invokes a result from a non-adjacent statement
    (e.g. "the bound established earlier", "by our construction", a shared
    constant or function defined three theorems back).
  • A statement's meaning depends on a definition or convention set up in
    another statement that is not referenced explicitly.

Inter-paper (only when a bibliography is provided):
  Only in pre_context or post_context.
  • A prose mention of an external result (e.g. "by the theorem of Smith–Jones",
    "using the estimate from [AuthorYear]") with NO \cite command, where the
    author's cite key appears in the bibliography. Extract dep_name
    (e.g. "Theorem 3.2") if visible.

━━━ Skip entirely — already captured ━━━

Skip any dependency that involves a \ref, \cref, \eqref, \autoref, \Cref,
\hyperref, or \cite command anywhere, that is signalled purely by a
proximity keyword ("above", "follows", "hence", "thus", "consequence", etc.)
next to an explicit label or cite, or that is a plain prose name matching a
known statement exactly (e.g. "Theorem 3.2", "Lemma 4.1"). Those are handled
by other methods.

━━━ Output ━━━
Respond with valid YAML only. Each entry is a compact array.

Intra entry:  [src_id, dep_id, location, key]
Inter entry:  [src_id, cite_key, dep_name_or_null, location, key]

  location: body | note | proof | pre_context | post_context (intra)
            pre_context | post_context (inter)
  key: 1–3 verbatim words from the text that signal the implicit dependency
       (e.g. "our construction", "the notation", "defined above")

deps:
- [src_id, dep_id, location, key]
cites:
- [src_id, cite_key, dep_name_or_null, location, key]

Omit a section entirely if empty.
